  "final": "`OrderHandler.list` now supports cursor pagination and returns `nextCursor`. `tsc -p tsconfig.json && node checks/public.cjs` passes, including the new checks I added.\n\n**`src/repository.ts`**\n- `list(tenant, after?)` filters by tenant first. If `after` is given, it then keeps only rows strictly after that `(createdAt, id)` pair.\n- It sorts ascending by `createdAt`, then `id`, using plain string comparison.\n- The filter returns a new array, so the input array and its rows are never mutated.\n- A cursor whose row was deleted, or which belongs to another tenant, still resumes correctly, because only the pair's position matters.\n\n**`src/handler.ts`**\n- The tenant and limit validation is unchanged.\n- If `cursor` is present, it must split on `|` into exactly two nonempty parts. Otherwise the handler throws `Error('invalid request')`. That includes an empty string cursor, because only `undefined` counts as no cursor.\n- `items` is the first `limit` rows after the cursor. `nextCursor` is `createdAt|id` of the last returned item if more matching rows remain, and `null` otherwise.\n- Requests without a cursor behave as before, with `nextCursor` now set when more rows exist.\n\n**`checks/public.cjs`**\n- I added checks for these cases:\n  - two-page traversal with equal dates;\n  - `nextCursor` being `null` on the last page;\n  - a cursor pair from another tenant;\n  - malformed cursors.",
